thread:
maria.cs: Quick sync: Tarpon account wants us to validate reserved capacity before the go-live on Thursday. Primary asks: quota priming, autoscale behavior under bursty traffic, and a clear rollback plan. Can we confirm owners?

lee.ops: I can handle quota changes and pool assignments. Will need pool name and expected peak RPS. Also, prefer a two-stage test: priming -> sustained -> spike -> soak.

devin.eng: I'll own the load harness + model variant matrix. Which model are we serving for Tarpon? Cold-starts were visible last month so we should do prefix cache priming.

maria.cs: Model: flowgen-11b-quant-cpu-fallback. Use reserved pool id "tarpon-pool-01". Expected steady: 40 RPS, spike to 160 RPS for 90s, target concurrency ~95. Priming plan: run top-20 prompts heat for 12 minutes, then drop to steady.

samira.e: Observability & quality here. I'll create a short-lived dashboard and push an eval probe to check for semantic drift during spike (we saw ~2% hallucination rise in prior rehearse). Alert thresholds: p95 latency > 600ms, error_rate > 0.8%, model_fallback_rate > 3%.

ops-bot: Calendar event created: Tarpon reserved rehearsal — 2024-03-14 09:30-11:00 PT. Reminder + oncall paging configured.

lee.ops: Quotas I'll set: reserved_replicas=6, burst_headroom=3, token_quota=1.2x baseline. Tagging the pool with customer=tarpon and stage=rehearsal. Will coordinate with infra to ensure GPU fallback lane is warmed.

devin.eng: Load script (k6) for spike stage below. Will run in staging first, then target the reserved pool via staging gateway with header x-reserved-pool: tarpon-pool-01.

```
# k6 spike profile (example)
import http from 'k6/http';
import { sleep } from 'k6';
export let options = {
  stages: [
    { duration: '10m', target: 40 },    // priming / steady
    { duration: '1m30s', target: 160 }, // spike
    { duration: '20m', target: 40 }     // soak
  ],
  thresholds: {
    http_req_duration: ['p(95)<600']
  }
};
export default function() {
  http.post(__ENV.TARGET_URL, JSON.stringify({ model: 'flowgen-11b-quant-cpu-fallback', prompt: 'Customer chat: help with billing' }), { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'Authorization': 'Bearer $TEST_KEY', 'x-reserved-pool': 'tarpon-pool-01' }});
  sleep(1);
}
```

maria.cs: Nice. Also: capture enablement notes — Tarpon asked for a short reference on "when to warm vs skip warm" and a plain example of token budgeting for conversational bursts. That's a good renewal-risk mitigator.

samira.e: I'll draft a 1-pager with examples and attach to the onboarding doc after the run.

devin.eng: Autoscale policy I'll test: min=3, max=18, scale_cooldown=45s, gpu_fallback_threshold=75%. If we hit GPU fallback, ensure fallback route uses quantized CPU variant and flag in logs.

lee.ops: Rollback plan: immediate traffic shift to shared-hosted lane + reduce reserved pool to zero if latencies or error rates cross thresholds for >2m. I'll open temp channel #tarpon-rehearsal-ops for real-time signals.

maria.cs: Who's taking notes for the postmortem and action items? Need one-liner bullet list including: anomalies, priming effectiveness, any token throttles, and customer-facing guidance.

devin.eng: I'll take notes. Will post an artifacts bundle (k6 results, traces, priming coverage) within 2 hours of rehearsal.

samira.e: If we detect a >1.5% quality regression during spike, we'll flag for model tuning and capture sample outputs to show Tarpon.

maria.cs: Great. Last ask: please add a quick cost estimate of the rehearsal (approx GPU-hours + extra tokens) and share with billing before handoff.

lee.ops: Noted. I'll ping finance and add estimated commit cost in the rehearsal notes.

maria.cs: Thanks team. I'll follow up with the final checklist after staging smoke run tonight. If anything urgent pops up, ping @oncall and @escalations in that order.
